# hermes-roam 0.5.3 — cron pause live proof (real Hermes 0.22.5, local backend)

# Two scratch homes (HOME_A, HOME_B) share one local-backend store (STORE) under
# WORK. Both opt into sync_cron. The transcript proves, in order:
#   1. HOME_A creates a live, ENABLED cron job with `hermes cron create`.
#   2. The plan-only pull lists the job as landing PAUSED with the exact
#      `hermes cron resume <id>` command and writes nothing.
#   3. `pull --apply` installs the job PAUSED (no next run), and
#      `hermes cron list` on HOME_B shows [paused].
#   4. The printed `hermes cron resume <id>` works on HOME_B.
#   5. HOME_A pulls back: its own copy stays [active]/enabled (0 files written).
#   6. HOME_B pushes its resumed store and HOME_A pulls it back: HOME_A's copy
#      stays [active] — the pause never round-trips to the origin.
#   7. While another process holds HOME_B's cron .jobs.lock, the pull fails
#      CLOSED inside the bounded wait (~31 s wall) with a clear error and
#      nothing written; after the lock is released the same pull succeeds and
#      the second job lands PAUSED too.
# Host paths are sanitized: WORK=the working directory, HOME_A/HOME_B=scratch homes,
# STORE=the local backend dir, HERMES_INSTALL=the Hermes install directory.
# The archive key never appears in this transcript.

